            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Sarah Jo"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good. I used an orange pineapple banana juice blend. My kids and I really liked it. I think next time, I'll add some strawberries. NOTE: This makes great healthy frozen pops, too. I just pour the smoothie mixture into dixie cups and stick in the freezer and when the mixture has firmed up, I stick in a popsicle stick."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "kathiemun"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I first fill my blender loosely with fresh spinach, blend with orange juice, then add sliced pieces of 1 frozen banana and 1/2 to 1 c blueberries. It's purple, and there is absolutely no taste of spinach!",
            "datePublished": "2011-07-18T15:27:16.680Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "jadun"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We loved it! The recipe is great as is, but I personally would increase the blueberries to 1 cup to pack in more nutrition.",
            "datePublished": "2017-01-07T01:37:41.663Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Annie'sKitchen"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I always throw my \"over ripe\" bananas in the freezer just for smoothies. Unfortunately, someone in my house used up the rest of them so I used 2 regular bananas which were not \"over ripe\" (the best for smoothies) and had no orange juice so I replaced with 1/2 plain yogurt and half vanilla flavored almond milk and everything else the same. Since I had no frozen bananas, I did add a few ice cubes to cool it. Next time I made it, I added more fresh blueberries, \"black\" frozen bananas and the same plain yogurt, almond milk, more vanilla and a bit more honey to feed my big eating family and they ALL LOVED IT!! Thanks for the recipe, it inspired me and the consistency was perfect.",
            "datePublished": "2014-11-15T15:09:23.117Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Amy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "A little too much banana, but otherwise very good. I made it again using only one banana and double the berries and it was perfect.",
            "datePublished": "2014-01-12T12:50:36.337Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "slmorton"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I only did half a cup of Orange juice and then added a half cup of vanilla-honey almond milk. Creamy &amp; Delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2015-06-11T06:22:05.917Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Janka"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I modified this recipe a bit based on other reviews and my fridge :-)\nI used 2 bananas, not frozen, about 1 cup of frozen blueberries, 1 cup of blueberry yoghurt (didn't have juice, but yoghurt), vanilla and 2 tablespoons oat meal.\nThis was my busy mom's (of an even busier toddler) lunch ;-) Now I have a new way of getting rid of dark bananas instead of muffins :-D\nThanks for the inspiration!",
            "datePublished": "2011-07-18T02:47:36.630Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "adrienneash"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was great, not too sweet and not too tart! My two year old drank a cup full and wanted more! I used 1/2 cup orange juice and 1/2 cup soymilk becuase my son can only have soy. Thanks for a great recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2011-03-20T10:58:20.090Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Grandma D"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good, however I did add blueberry Greek yogurt to add protein and to make it creamier. Any yogurt will work.",
            "datePublished": "2015-12-31T08:24:21.960Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Lynn"
            },
            "reviewBody": "First time I ever made a smoothy, and it was great. I followed the suggestion to use yogurt instead of oj. I made half the recipe, so I used a 6 oz. vanilla lite, one bannana, 1/4 c blueberries and about 1/2 c milk. It was thick and delicious. No need for honey or vanilla. It made two 8 oz. servings.",
            "datePublished": "2010-05-24T15:08:19.617Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "lorimca"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We double the liquid in this, using water mostly to cut costs. We double the fruit, often using 1/2 strawberries as well. We also add a handful of SPINACH! Yes, spinach, you cannot taste or see it, the color stays purple (or really dark rusty red if you use strawberries) and it gets some greens into us painfully as we never get any otherwise. Whole family loves it, even knowing there's spinach in it. If you double the fruit and use frozen strawberries as well, you don't need to freeze the bananas. Edited to add: Obviously I meant it's a *painless* way to get some greens in. LOL.",
            "datePublished": "2010-04-01T05:27:05.910Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was ok, but I found using frozen bananas was far more hassle than it was worth. Room temp bananas still add thickness to the smoothie, and the frozen berries add the chill. If I make this again, I won’t bother freezing my bananas beforehand.",
            "datePublished": "2025-04-09T06:50:09.447Z"
          },
